2. Personal noncommercial use

You may install, run, study, and make reasonable copies of the Software solely
as an individual acting on your own behalf for personal noncommercial purposes.
Examples include unpaid personal applications, tools, websites, hobby projects,
personal study, and private experimentation without an intended commercial
application.

Personal noncommercial use excludes use for or on behalf of a business,
employer, client, nonprofit, association, government, or other organization.
It also excludes use intended to obtain commercial advantage, compensation,
or revenue, whether directly or indirectly. In particular, this license does
not authorize:

  a. use in business applications, websites, online shops, paid membership
     services, or projects that promote paid products, services, or
     professional engagements;
  b. use in applications, websites, or services containing paid advertising or
     affiliate links, or that solicit or receive donations or other
     fundraising payments;
  c. paid development, installation, maintenance, hosting packages specifically
     offering the Software, consulting, or other client work involving it;
  d. selling, renting, licensing for a fee, or commercially bundling the
     Software, or offering it as a paid or advertising-supported service; or
  e. use within an organization's operations, even without profit or a charge
     to its users.

Paying ordinary domain, general-purpose hosting, and connectivity costs does
not by itself make your otherwise eligible project commercial. A general-
purpose infrastructure provider may make the technical copies and execute the
Software necessary to host your eligible project solely at your direction;
this limited permission does not authorize the provider to offer the Software
as a product or managed software service. An optional donation to the Licensor
does not make your own use commercial.

Commercial or organizational use requires separate written permission from the
Licensor before that use begins. This license provides no commercial trial or
automatic exception based on low revenue, small size, or charitable status.

3. Permission to modify and meaning of Modification

You may modify the Software for a purpose permitted by section 2, subject to
sections 4 and 5. A Modification is a substantive change, addition, deletion,
translation, or adaptation of covered code, documentation, or assets, including
work incorporating or adapting any protected part of the Software.

Modifications include private, experimental, unfinished, and later abandoned
changes. You need not preserve or submit every transient editor state; your
submission must capture all substantive changes, including substantive work
that you later remove or abandon. You may group related changes for submission,
provided each change meets its applicable deadline.

Changing ordinary Software settings or runtime configuration is not a
Modification. Neither are user content, application data, database records,
credentials, caches, backups, or output merely processed or generated by the
Software. This output exclusion does not exempt compiled, bundled, minified,
or otherwise transformed versions of the Software itself, or output that copies
or adapts its protected expression.

Independently written code that only interoperates with the Software and does
not copy or adapt its protected expression is also excluded. These exclusions
do not exempt actual changes to the Software's source code or covered assets.

4. Mandatory contribution to the Original Project

For each Modification, you must deliver its complete, editable source to the
Licensor by the earlier of:

  a. 30 calendar days after you first save that substantive change; or
  b. the first time you distribute a copy containing it to another person.

This obligation applies even if you never distribute the Modification and use
it only on your personal devices, applications, or websites. Continuing
development, reverting, or abandoning a change does not restart or cancel its
deadline.

Follow the contribution instructions that the Licensor supplies for this
Software in its accompanying documentation, official project website, or
Original Project. Submit through the designated channel, such as a supported
pull request, or deliver through the Licensor's contact channel identified in
those materials. Merely publishing a fork or an unrelated download, without
submitting or delivering the changes to the Licensor, does not satisfy this
obligation. If no contribution or contact channel is identified, the procedure
for unavailable channels below applies; you need not guess a delivery
destination.

Include a usable patch against an identified project version or the modified
source files with sufficient context, a concise description of the changes,
and any build instructions or additional original source needed to understand
and use them. Identify authorship and third-party material accurately. Confirm
that you have the authority to grant the rights in section 5. No fee or
acceptance by the Licensor is required for a valid submission.

Do not submit credentials, signing keys, private application data, personal
information unnecessary to the patch, or material you have no right to
disclose. Replace deployment-specific secrets and personal values with safe
examples while preserving the substantive change. This is not permission to
withhold the actual fix.

For a security-sensitive change, use a private reporting channel designated by
the Licensor. If none is available, make a non-sensitive request for a private
delivery method through a documented project or Licensor contact channel.
Such a request does not require publicly disclosing the vulnerability or its
exploit details.

If no submission or contact channel is identified, an identified channel is
unavailable, or private delivery is needed but no private channel has been
supplied, keep a dated record of the missing instructions, your attempted
delivery, or your request, as applicable, and retain a submission-ready copy.
The delivery deadline is suspended only while that obstacle prevents safe
delivery. Check the documented contribution/contact locations at least every
30 days and deliver within 14 days after you learn, or would learn through
those required checks, that safe delivery is available. The record of your
timely check, attempt, or request satisfies the submission condition during
this suspension, including for otherwise permitted redistribution of the
modified Software. Do not publicly disclose sensitive details merely to meet
a deadline.

A binding legal prohibition on disclosing particular material is preserved
under section 9. Deliver a lawful, sanitized patch wherever possible; notify
the Licensor of any remaining obstacle to the extent lawful. This exception
does not excuse a restriction that you voluntarily undertake to evade these
terms. Do not incorporate material for which you cannot grant section 5 rights.

